---> What is the difference between == and ===?
| Operator | Name            | Checks type? | Example               |
| -------- | --------------- | ------------ | --------------------- |
| `==`     | Loose equality  | ❌ No         | `5 == "5"` → `true`   |
| `===`    | Strict equality | ✅ Yes        | `5 === "5"` → `false` |

console.log(5 == "5"); //true
5 is a number and "5" is a string, but == converts the string to a number before comparing.

console.log(5 === "5"); //false
5    → number
"5"  → string

"== is the loose equality operator. It compares values after type conversion when necessary. === is the strict equality operator, which compares both value and data type without type conversion. In general, I prefer using === because it avoids unexpected type coercion."
== → value
=== → value + type


---> What is the difference between null and undefined in JavaScript?
|                 | `undefined`                     | `null`                                        |
| --------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Meaning         | Value has **not been assigned** | Intentionally set to **no value**             |
| Usually happens | Automatically                   | Explicitly by the developer                   |
| Type            | `"undefined"`                   | `"object"` *(historical JavaScript behavior)* |
| Example         | `let x;`                        | `let x = null;`                               |

A variable is undefined when it has been declared but no value has been assigned.
let name;
console.log(name);

null means the developer intentionally says there is no value. (object)
let name = null;
console.log(name);